      "id": "legendre-1785",
      "why": "Establishes the orthogonality and real, interior zeros of the Legendre polynomials that provide the required node polynomial properties."
    },
    {
      "id": "trefethen-2013",
      "why": "Modern treatment that includes the proof an n-point rule cannot be exact for the square of its node polynomial and so cannot exceed degree 2n−1."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Gauss constructed an n‑point quadrature rule whose nodes are the zeros of the n-th Legendre polynomial and which integrates every polynomial of degree ≤ 2n−1 exactly [gauss-1814]. Jacobi’s orthogonality derivation explains that the node polynomial must be orthogonal to all lower-degree polynomials (hence its zeros are the Legendre zeros) and shows degree 2n cannot be attained by any n‑node rule [jacobi-1826; legendre-1785]. Modern expositions restate and streamline these arguments, including the impossibility of reaching degree 2n because the square of the node polynomial has positive integral yet is annihilated by the rule [trefethen-2013]."
}
